00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.350
Leah: Let’s first start by looking
at the origins of open source code.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.369
The idea of making source code open
and freely available, originated in

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.910
1983 with an ideological movement.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.159
Richard Stallman a programmer from
MIT, was the informal founder, who

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.920
believed that software should be
accessible to programmers to modify as

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.110
they wished to hopefully improve it.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:28.450
He released free code under his own
license under the GNU public license.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:33.170
Jack: This ideology and new
approach eventually led to the Open

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.850
Source Initiative [OSI] in 1998.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.890
The OSI acts as an informational and
governing repository of Open source

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:47.610
software providing rules and guidelines
on how to use it, as well as providing

00:00:47.639 --> 00:00:49.930
code licensing information and support.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.369
Leah: Open source code is usually
stored in a public repository

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.470
and shared with the public.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.250
Anyone can access it and use the code
independently or contribute to the

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:02.550
design or functionality of the project.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.360
Programmers who have access to it can
change a program by adding to it or

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.460
fixing parts that aren't working properly.

00:01:10.459 --> 00:01:15.380
Jack: Open source software [OSS] usually
comes with a distribution license

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:20.490
including terms of how developers can use,
study, modify and distribute the software.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:25.349
When the source code is changed the
OSS must include what was altered as

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.369
well as the methods used to alter it.

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.950
Leah: Some well known examples of OSS are:

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.930
GNU/Linux-operating system

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.260
Mozilla Firefox- web browser

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.720
GIMP- photo editing suite

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:44.989
Open Office- office suite with word
processing, spreadsheets, and databases

00:01:46.379 --> 00:01:50.990
Advantages with OSS are that it is
free and comes with a large community

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:54.800
of users and programmers that
continuously modify the software.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:59.360
This source code is publicly
available and also opens up

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.219
opportunities for new programmers.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:06.490
The disadvantages are it can have
compatibility issues and by it's nature

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:11.700
it has problems with quality, performance
and security flaws, but the large groups

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:16.090
of programming and coding contributors
identify and fix these issues.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.019
Jack: Let’s now have a
look at Closed source code.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.890
Closed software [proprietary] containing
closed source code is owned by commercial

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:28.700
organisations with creators having
copyright over whatever they create.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:33.549
But source code can be easy to copy
and take apart to see how it works.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.720
The solution to this is by not
publishing the source code but instead

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.780
to compile it into a set of machine
code instructions in an executable

00:02:41.789 --> 00:02:43.890
file and sell that to customers.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:49.260
This is closed source software as it
is difficult for anyone to work out

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.440
what the original source code was by
just referring to the executable file.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:58.100
Leah: Commercial organisations invest
a great deal of money on developing

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:01.870
their software and they want to make
it profitable therefore making it

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.739
closed source is a better option.

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:08.399
They will also make it more sophisticated
to attract people to buy it rather than

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:12.529
download open source equivalents which
don't have the same sophistication.

00:03:13.719 --> 00:03:16.420
Jack: Some well known examples
of closed source programs are:

00:03:16.779 --> 00:03:19.440
Microsoft Windows – the
Windows operating system

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.150
Apple macOS – the Apple operating system

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.549
Adobe Photoshop – Adobe’s closed
source photo-editing suite

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.839
Microsoft office – Microsoft’s
word processing, spreadsheet,

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:32.779
and database software

